speechtext:
The reply from the Department of Regional Economic Expansion is as follows: (a) Projects initiated under the Canada-Newfoundland Gros Morne Park Area Development Subsidiary Agreement, signed on May 28, 1974: (1) Tourism Studies; (2) Regional Planning and Land Use Studies; (3) Inshore Sports Fishing Study; (4) Rocky Harbour Water and Sewer Systems; (5) Norris Point/ Neddy Harbour Water and Sewer Systems; (6) Cow Head Water and Sewer Systems; (7) Trout River Water and Sewer Systems; (8) Woody Point Water System Extension; (9) Solid Waste Disposal Study and construction: Norris Point-Rocky Harbour, Cow Head area, Woody Point area; (10) Access Road route 430 to Rocky Harbour; (11) Reconstruction and paving Norris Point to Rocky Harbour; (12) Highway 430 reconstruction St. Paul's to Shallow Bay; (13) Paving main road in Cow Head; (14) Bridge construction-Crousher's Gulch, (b) The amendment to this agreement, signed April 22, 1977, provides funding for the continuation and completion of the following projects, as originally identified by the agreement; (1) Tourism Studies; (2) Regional Planning and Land Use Studies; (3) Rocky Harbour Water and Sewer Systems;
(4)	Norris Point/Neddy Harbour Water and Sewer Systems;
(5)	Trout River Water and Sewer Systems; (6) Woody Point Water System Extension; (7) Solid Waste Disposal Study and construction: Norris Point-Rocky Harbour, Cow Head area,
July 5, 1977

Woody Point area. The Gros Morne Park Area Development Subsidiary Agreement is implemented under the direction of a federal-provincial management committee. Work to be carried out this summer will include continued activity towards completion of projects identified in section (b); in addition, the management committee will approve other projects on an on-going basis for implementation during the summer.
